    "content": "Ethiopian Airlines is scheduled to resume flights between Addis Ababa and Mekelle on Sunday, according to reports. The route had been suspended due to security concerns in the Tigray region.\n\nThe decision to restart the flights follows recent security developments in Mekelle, including reports that forces of the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF) and allied groups have withdrawn from the city. Subsequently, the Ethiopian National Defense Forces (ENDF) and allied militias known as the Tigray Peace Forces (TPF) have reportedly taken control.\n\nThis route is vital for connecting the Tigray region with the rest of Ethiopia and beyond, facilitating travel for business, family visits, and humanitarian efforts. The resumption of flights is seen as a positive step toward normalizing transportation links after a period of uncertainty.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom travel frequently between East Africa and other regions, the resumption of this route could ease travel logistics, particularly for those with connections in Ethiopia or who use Addis Ababa as a transit hub. It may also facilitate the delivery of humanitarian aid to Tigray, where many Somalis have extended family ties.\n\nThe restart of Ethiopian Airlines flights is expected to proceed as planned, although the situation remains fluid. Passengers are advised to check with the airline for the latest updates.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/10/duulimaadyada-addis-ababa-iyo-mekelle-oo-dib-u-billaabanaya/",
