    "id": "news-1791632217998-11",
    "title": "Somalia Prepares to Implement IATA Billing and Settlement Plan After Successful Trial",
    "original_title": "Soomaaliya oo Isu Diyaarinaysa Hirgelinta Nidaamka Lacag-bixinta Duulimaadyada IATA BSP",
    "slug": "somalia-prepares-to-implement-iata-billing-and-settlement-plan-after-successful-trial",
    "summary": "The Somali Civil Aviation Authority (SCAA) announced that Somalia is preparing for full implementation of the IATA Billing and Settlement Plan (BSP) after a successful trial phase. The move aims to streamline ticket sales and financial settlements between airlines and IATA-accredited travel agencies.",
    "content": "The Somali Civil Aviation Authority (SCAA) has announced that Somalia is preparing to fully implement the International Air Transport Association (IATA) Billing and Settlement Plan (BSP), following the successful completion of a trial phase. The trial, which began in April, involved four travel agencies and several airlines. The BSP is a system that centralizes the sale of tickets and financial settlements between airlines and IATA-accredited travel agencies.\n\nThe BSP reduces the need for multiple separate financial transfers by consolidating sales data and funds into a single centralized system. This simplifies the process for airlines and agencies, potentially lowering costs and improving efficiency. The trial phase was designed to test the system's functionality and readiness before a nationwide rollout.\n\nSomalia's civil aviation sector has been rebuilding after decades of conflict. The SCAA has been working to align the country's aviation standards with international norms, and the adoption of the BSP is seen as a key step in that direction. The successful trial indicates that local travel agencies and airlines are prepared for the transition.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the implementation of the BSP could have significant implications. Many diaspora members frequently purchase tickets for relatives in Somalia or travel themselves between Somalia and other countries. A more streamlined ticketing and settlement system could lead to more reliable bookings, transparent pricing, and improved service from travel agencies. It may also facilitate easier remittances for travel-related expenses.\n\nThe full implementation of the BSP is expected to enhance Somalia's integration into the global aviation financial system. This could boost confidence among international airlines looking to operate in Somalia and encourage more travel agencies to seek IATA accreditation. The SCAA has not announced a specific date for the full rollout but indicated that preparations are underway.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/10/soomaaliya-oo-isu-diyaarinaysa-hirgelinta-nidaamka-lacag-bixinta-duulimaadyada-iata-bsp/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
    "source_name": "Goobjoog",
    "original_url": "https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/10/soomaaliya-oo-isu-diyaarinaysa-hirgelinta-nidaamka-lacag-bixinta-duulimaadyada-iata-bsp/",
